# Dekker's product: TwoProd without an FMA, the constant-evaluation variant of two_prod. It requires
# that no intermediate overflows. The underflow term (13/8) eta is the proved one (the source has
# 7/2); exhaustive runs on toy formats approach it.
kernel:        two_prod_dekker
input:         a
input:         b
output:        r = p e
value:         a*b
ensures:       leading_rounded r
ensures:       error r R c=0 k=2 t=13/8
ensures:       error r R c=0 k=2 t=0 when=product_large
min_precision: 4
theorem:       proofs/theories/TwoProdDekker.v two_prod_dekker_correct
source:        Dekker's product with Veltkamp's split (Dekker 1971); overflow preconditions BM11
license:       algorithm: published; proof: MIT
status:        proved
cone_exempt:   the splits of a and b can overflow while p = RN(ab) is finite, which is why the kernel requires that no intermediate overflows; it runs in constant evaluation, where an overflow or a NaN is not a constant expression (GCC and Clang reject the program)

(ah, al) = split(a)
(bh, bl) = split(b)
p  = prod(a, b)
q0 = prod(ah, bh)
t1 = sub(q0, p)
q1 = prod(ah, bl)
t2 = add(t1, q1)
q2 = prod(al, bh)
t3 = add(t2, q2)
q3 = prod(al, bl)
e  = add(t3, q3)
